[{"id":"VRCE-6-80-1","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":1,"english":"Then the counsellors of Pulastya's son, hearing of Indrajit having been slain, speedily conveyed the tidings to the Ten-necked one, who was ignorant* of the same.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.609,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080001","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c1791d59b1dc33166c565840cb872fb5de0059da132d913177a0fdc4daa6c775","compact":"6a522a4eac9ea16a022019cf897a10a12335c1c05a362d0d91eda739e5ba2e43","nasal":"df0b480f0f85b4d11b6ecb3537fed8bd1595e6ac47890c9474e23957a3e7f6ac"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-2","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":2,"english":"O mighty monarch, in our very sight your exceedingly effulgent son has been slain by Lakş maņa with the help of Vibhīşaņa.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.981,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080002","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b5529f6172d7e223736f0d4c001d4112de895b969f99f1b135b59e6aac2e4f14","compact":"0e1f6e42fdf913d579a68493755556692bd2789a6d6508138bd4a8fc2a903763","nasal":"0e1f6e42fdf913d579a68493755556692bd2789a6d6508138bd4a8fc2a903763"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-3","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":3,"english":"That hero confronted by the (other) heros, has been vanquished in fight. And the hero, you son, the conqueror of the celestial chief, has also been slain by Lakşmaņa. And having gratified Lakş maņa with his shafts, he has repaired to the supreme regions.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.859,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080003","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"76c42cdce5c5c1038366fb29e293a15772ad915fd313e20e89baefc2f1070f1e","compact":"1652375d11accd4a0053dbeabcad3330d52ae16f424bc9473d7abdbd42943786","nasal":"d0d8490dff66af645957327e1cd9b2226c143c411ae7497ae74b60e9e927bc8a"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-4","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":4,"english":"Hearing of that mighty terror, viz., the rueful and dreadful destruction of his son, \"Indrajit, in battle, he straightway swooned away.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080004","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"283b1c6e694793d60deff0142ad2e19e778a8de2c2e61e8dd5830d41e889da17","compact":"607d6009ab914ca3ac40579d4a139dad3542977a7e536dc6c8795e49d428dccb","nasal":"a64b29839f0b8ac9c8242dda972f34c5af08275a10ecb6765bc8e815432c77ac"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-5","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":5,"english":"Then regaining his consciousness after a long while, that foremost of Räkşasas-the kingover-whelmed with grief for his son, and distressed, burst into lamentations, with his senses wildered, thus.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.697,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080005","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2b03a8b21b2d11a4036c4a818270799cae65789179bd56857835b3c8fb44a6d4","compact":"7cf289e7a58002c70ed3540812df6ea4f105b3128720dbf690106b16fc101436","nasal":"891d6d18e20bae35b0d7955e13070b438881023445b199f0e72428343558e3e9"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-6","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":6,"english":"O foremost in the Räkşasa army! O my child endowed with high might! Having conquered Indra himself, wherefore today have you come under the sway of Lakşmaņa?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.845,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080006","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8be96044b8bfa408f798c468d11a504e906e8c6a9751dac02bd1128ddc8621c2","compact":"42dacb487b1615db6deda5255ccfbf6dcbf7510a9d548be57a616ee176ed592b","nasal":"8c99a9678330ff991a852ad98ea47fa6a8d89f8236649a0541963b75a4b41a9e"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-7","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":7,"english":"Doubtless, you enraged could pierce with you arrows the Destroyer of Time himself, and the summit of Mandāra, what shall I say of Laks mana in encounted?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.822,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080007","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ad710c6f27005d953a336d3ad543bb5a8bfd8b9c7db210daf8b93dc90c044868","compact":"e06b059437342bacba032b05fb8967c9ebc2a95c46ebd03cdea3e6ac91ff05f2","nasal":"2a95b8c51f14d33a0ef2daed279e9709e944329a5748ed31fcb2b02abad789b0"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-8","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":8,"english":"Today king Vivasvata has raised himself immensely in my estimation, for today he, O mighty-armed one, has brought you under the law of Time.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080008","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"23f2b7a65671e1c2d5ddaa817e93002bf3297ceee0cd1f97b50741fcb4845739","compact":"9962dd047aa444d579ff4e9d999fd4fa28737ff040c4c165307c0c2fd2069f01","nasal":"9962dd047aa444d579ff4e9d999fd4fa28737ff040c4c165307c0c2fd2069f01"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-9","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":9,"english":"But this is the way of capable warriors as well as celestials. He that lay down his life for his lord, repair to heaven.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080009","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0e3f9b13e72415eb2ffb52fa8b2245ef4d073c132b0f73f2c6b86001cf02bf78","compact":"85c8316a15ba7cc3fc2d9c00a271fd8724c4393aa3ab1aa8146e9b25d19f5633","nasal":"c1dd4f7b730cccb59c1ae07c5aad4d34b03bf0d9441379295899d7499d2841b2"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-10","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":10,"english":"To-day seeing Indrajit slain, the deities and the guardians of the worlds, and the mighty saints, their fear gone off, shall sleep peacefully.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.843,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080010","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d08696ee3fa014cb188975e2c02d1ea4087984133a40b157a0a8b4ad0f80ea06","compact":"d8c435f336a027d62431932816205517156f348909134200d696e6b4e5bf8fc9","nasal":"99dff06d7bfe5d8ac488413035e0eed223fb45f55a900765a719d6cd83b91b95"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-11","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":11,"english":"This triple world and this entire earth with her woods, reft of one—Indrajit—appear to me as vacant.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.758,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080011","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"97c7ffe800c8e8910604925b3db486d8f2966e3e06888039de9436e0fda80743","compact":"4b12d88c22135ea2e85e135ff288c21a21ba1ba9a9192a33115f6bfe5ac7ebcd","nasal":"1479f0281125281fbf806ef1f354fb3b68b1101e946fc15fbff85e41acb1e930"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-12","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":12,"english":"To-day shall I hear the cries of the daughters of the Naiſtas in my inner apartment, like to the roars of she-elephants in a mountain-cavern.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.635,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080012","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ee4686bdeb7a6050652fd87807a496428a42b81b556e32c315005babe71d602c","compact":"cc9ce6153285e8da024e24b734cd29c03afc0423b529676c552c9da7142077fb","nasal":"6428648bfb84abd4f69c7a523298c09a0e01092a6e76cfb70a0085e8729418fa"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-13","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":13,"english":"Renouncing your state of an heir-apparent, and Lanka and Raksasas, O subduer of enemies, and your mother, and me, and your wifewhither have you gone, (leaving) us all?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.857,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080013","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"27f2f284089f0f08c45bcc7b5b05cfcec5577c0d6385f06388f9a483f00a52a5","compact":"24f3830ba9d22703c2a8874b593dbfadaae80d71e02750d4a5cecfe79074c184","nasal":"3cac07707ffdd762fee0cd7db9c0b8fce2a1262c27396de3acbb8079299c31db"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-14","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":14,"english":"O hero, I having gone to the region of Yama, you ought to have performed my last rites, but you stand in a relation the very reverse of this.* *i.e. I have to perform your last rites, you having fallen in fight while I am alive.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080014","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"557dec70aea3f79db7b3b15b63b50b0d600bc29b66d9b26e75dd48964023e650","compact":"2ccd20e8543f324dc450a26fa01691aad30e03f4c70d9e3b086fed02d8dc3f1e","nasal":"ef7c2c2b89895c9041238ad8f1882f3dc122aed85ef2f87f9211fdb92969fa4b"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-15","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":15,"english":"And Sugriva living and Lakşmana and Raghava, without extracting my dart, whither have you gone, leaving us all?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.456,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080015","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2c9d6fbf86c72d78dbc43185daffff8bd18c4962e3125f7bc333cfdf4b98b26e","compact":"9cdbdfeb7c941784d9fadf3ba824d8a57b8c048b435dc60db065e1d240aa91c3","nasal":"9cdbdfeb7c941784d9fadf3ba824d8a57b8c048b435dc60db065e1d240aa91c3"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-16","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":16,"english":"Then arising from the disaster that had befallen his son, a mighty wrath overcame the lord of Räkşasas-Rāvaņa, as he was thus indulging in lamentations. And even as in summer, the (Solar) rays flame up the Sun, the destruction of his son flamed up all the fiercer, him, who was naturally irascible.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.804,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080016","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b557cd51aaed3a834ce252905973065b4e5efe2d2a82f42065db13fae1012f59","compact":"c533dbd46e9520d580d960e4d207b69f11d3523d3c586e1a0e171c66e9995b8e","nasal":"c533dbd46e9520d580d960e4d207b69f11d3523d3c586e1a0e171c66e9995b8e"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-17","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":17,"english":"And his form, terrific by nature, in consequence of the fire of his wrath having possessed it entirely, resembled the form of the enraged Rudra.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.583,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080017","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"fd17af4586307f2132e9272705cebeb7831b64444a35c515136bba0a027c3d73","compact":"014c7fd53cbbc9a27e56deb296d6cc6f210cf39bdf4780308f7713a37c5c9c47","nasal":"3d07be6fbf004e1c231aa32ea952ab0288abb8bc608bbfe72a81d14fdbdaccae"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-18","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":18,"english":"And from the eyes of that infuriated one fell drops of tears as drop from a flaming lamp drops of burning oil.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.481,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080018","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3ffba3bc36e5f7e4bee4fa12a186a19e994c321a04297ed3e9c330dc4ed848fa","compact":"255a7acf4fa204be4876bd13c57b901490cecda71a092af5f8d67b9908f7fda6","nasal":"6dcc3b14e550fea91b757a27466f61d0ae583ef3fbf3751eb7ccaf09c843251e"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-19","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":19,"english":"As the grinded his teeth, the gnashing resembled the sounds sent by the machine* drawn by the Dänavas and churing the deep. *Meaning the mountain Mandara.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.639,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080019","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bde36580ab920c5feca666c40c5d8bbb89785a2cd9862515f5aead8bd8c51e06","compact":"7ce3476439e6d4635b6b03f1b2e3642f64c19f6f448cc7e4ee838648ed1514d9","nasal":"29807dcf54421be3c81fe9a0f5ff01733cbc47ea6134f3d070f45c4a16f1651f"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-20","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":20,"english":"The Rakşasas durst not approach that one eyeing round and resembling the Destroyer himself, intent on devouring both mobile and immobile.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.901,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080020","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1b30b40354860cb1e81479424510b296fa78ceabce87c5cfc119b0ee384e4f7f","compact":"895c2951bdf08bfac6836a57742c24b1cca80e5cfcdef5d47eed1e5a28925a58","nasal":"895c2951bdf08bfac6836a57742c24b1cca80e5cfcdef5d47eed1e5a28925a58"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-21","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":21,"english":"The Rakşasas durst not approach that one eyeing round and resembling the Destroyer himself, intent on devouring both mobile and immobile.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080021","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d7c1a312ff63d8da5ea47382195c849b042235494332f04750a35e3e0c0c6ca5","compact":"037dec49efda30b28c57e02ed2a2ba4a835cff437c783f2d7b9750bf01c90954","nasal":"24342ee94898a7aa4cfbf9d9c4ad8243284df80f270d2dbc943bdc02a44ace11"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-22","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":22,"english":"Then exceedingly enraged, Ravana-lord of Rākşasas-spoke in the midst of the Räkşasas, inciting them to battle.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080022","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e86e6b3b397f298e99ea613779a23644d45197e126a9c6029fa542f1e7204f0e","compact":"14b8f6a0a942c2b7607d722599bb4ff698755cc3106a02a4e1902c0b40c04b66","nasal":"eeffe1d2a14f4becd909ac7d7d48ac5fcf3715c027835f7bc474a5f288371e0b"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-23","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":23,"english":"I practised severe austerities for thousands of years; and gratified the Self-sprung at times.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.627,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080023","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"49e9ae5816f0ca729d219f4235ee8000b912f72aad71b0ac7bec11115674e535","compact":"b93885733117dfd09c25490434f561453a7b09e00bc1184aa19df37cf1cecd11","nasal":"0978241db2c829e3bde121a75bae24f1c732fa2be21d980460569c3768a9b76b"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-24","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":24,"english":"Neither from Asuras nor from celestials can any fear spring to me.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.726,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-26"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080024","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2f9db5dd2ba551ead2d2c7e84c3bf45acb557bd6779c9cf69108e5d1d463527a","compact":"73ca796d3739b7567952cece74ebc276e6a8c78cb8bb4328c09c9941c229f368","nasal":"e91f65bf1b98ee198b86a3045f49b0db32a42d3c4dbe4cf217ed2d8b9c34b28f"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-25","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":25,"english":"My mail conferred on me by Brahma, which in effulgence resembles the Sun himself, was not broken by blows like to thunder-bolt at the war between the deities and demons.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.738,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-27"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080025","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"edccfa8f3778e5f941e38934aedec80406e513b756b161f89f7907aed18a3e9d","compact":"e284dfbb384ecc785f2e632def730b1f22b554c8508288bcec38d17f67032c35","nasal":"665bb657c681fe337835a4f061d8a70b2f3888c6dce3aab04bf20f57dd5e4bd5"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-26","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":26,"english":"Today who will come forward against me in battle accoutred in that and mounted on a car in the conflict? Not even Purandara himself.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.933,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-28"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080026","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"08196fecf819af1719ebae7d3b618a255a8c35f6381aada425978e07a24d8dfe","compact":"0a6e66440ab4ded2367abc1352c7c88ffa8def52013e84012d8a5b1bf36a383b","nasal":"0a6e66440ab4ded2367abc1352c7c88ffa8def52013e84012d8a5b1bf36a383b"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-27","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":27,"english":"Do you, beating hundreds of drums, today raise that mighty and dreadful bow of mine which, with arrows, had been bestowed on one by the gratified Self-sprung at the war of the gods and Asuras, so that I may therewith compass the destruction of Rama and Lakşmaņa in mighty battle.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.898,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-29"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080027","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"257f96b7787d8952c61d1fe442ec5bb7afba464a9208674671ce9d0565a08f61","compact":"be93fdecf3691268c6e22b0d45b798d828d258bedc774f8dcf49a8cad1b81aac","nasal":"01d357f703ac95cd25c2df5cf3b0db6b71545d5e944726da376f779691a69432"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-28","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":28,"english":"Do you, beating hundreds of drums, today raise that mighty and dreadful bow of mine which, with arrows, had been bestowed on one by the gratified Self-sprung at the war of the gods and Asuras, so that I may therewith compass the destruction of Rama and Lakşmaņa in mighty battle.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.821,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-29"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080028","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0a147e86aa52261fcd0c3adeb6faad9780972890382e7176797004e9a3689c94","compact":"1453d49062b1664fb4da457d1a34f9bb7e04528822da5ab5e26bf418607545b0","nasal":"1453d49062b1664fb4da457d1a34f9bb7e04528822da5ab5e26bf418607545b0"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-29","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":29,"english":"Burning (in grief) for the destruction of his son, the fell Råvana. coming under the influence of anger, reflection (in his mind), resolved to slay Sīta.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.873,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-30"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080029","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3330ce0a72f894f2dc83da0eb7b59cb852592ac44754ff50be45eeaeee5eef79","compact":"c77b27c00ee57b25fb4e2453ce3954163a519d373016a17f08f24a0b5be0ba67","nasal":"9428b4f6ddd963105eee7d37cf0d2e5c56263f7ff1fa79b2c9bed7dbad529582"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-30","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":30,"english":"And eyeing round, that copper-eyed and dreadful one of a terrific aspect, distressed, in a piteous voice addressed all the night-rangers, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.726,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-31"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080030","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f519c56997504b30c243b141bd55842724b2a6d173006b2eba4dfd8b55a5e473","compact":"46a88c4242dd2b4cf346ce1c2d5f347efee4c16fc40e6e7e5aab3d3cfeb331ea","nasal":"2c7d24f4fabd24a4768bb2d387738ccf7c6656ca1c14652da0f9bcb5c49d7919"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-31","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":31,"english":"By his power of illusion, my beloved child, for deceiving the wood-rangers, slaying something (else), had shown it to them as Sītā.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.857,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-32"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080031","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e5c10c27a5c83b29b521a3441668f60a72f9fc9269e7e5aaeeff4060934434e0","compact":"b122805440d24c32812ff4711a8dc7cc063b1fa1842597abc9efa1ed7f39bec0","nasal":"a5b1c6d9ad42fafab8fe932c7f5811e76d0c889513071f22d4498aed58638127"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-32","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":32,"english":"Now will I do that good office myself, Vaidehi will I slay-even her that is devoted to that friend of Ksatriyas.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.854,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-33"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080032","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"167cbe24c91ca4e3005118bdc9accdd44112ed4d26e54ebacc1273011cced830","compact":"38586187293cb6170d2b1c626373c962a40c4c343e2764fc6cd1e7aebf3b2ae8","nasal":"fc7bec0337c4d226ae46475cfdb4895f60d8e7f9bae449bdbede1c8e6e2966db"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-33","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":33,"english":"Having spoken thus to his counsellors he swiftly took up a sword; and raising it, of excellent temper and of cerulean hue, he rushed out vehemently, surrounded by his wives and counsellors. From grief for his son. Råvana with his consciousness wilderd, taking up a sword; in rage, rushed towards the place where Mithila's daughter was.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.614,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-34"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080033","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"03d7d9958c1a04c3bbe831a6026179257403841a334ebdef