worlds conquered; and many other foes were brought down in battle.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-36"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080036","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"04e96aa0de51ce5101304ef76556ffbd73203ef799e2da471a5f5a39fee39a63","compact":"74d0f5191a461cc4b10263280dd1a0437cfaa714848bc65db95ebf3eaa1a0af9","nasal":"74d0f5191a461cc4b10263280dd1a0437cfaa714848bc65db95ebf3eaa1a0af9"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-37","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":37,"english":"As they were speaking thus, Ravana, reft of his senses by wrath, rushed against Vaidehi, who had taken up her abode in the Asoka wood.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.933,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-38"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080037","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"aa785efe6b4752e46ffd2a146e52ddb0a4d98385156717cd0b61cccf0ed8a73f","compact":"930fd33d667c9233d28faaf5cdeb3d5d4e9a1b297b37a99199e7bd59732f0be6","nasal":"930fd33d667c9233d28faaf5cdeb3d5d4e9a1b297b37a99199e7bd59732f0be6"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-38","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":38,"english":"And dissuaded by his friends endowed with good intelligence, he wrought up with high wrath, darted (against Sītā), even as an enraged planet' rush against Rohinr in the firmament. 1. Kuja etc. 2. Hesperus.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.9,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-39"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080038","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b33c5d7d801e75a5aa63c11ff9e18678a8e6cb07e423918117d97ece30768579","compact":"9efed0a430c48ff97a804f394df099bf384f3764c3da189635108bb287c3730b","nasal":"9efed0a430c48ff97a804f394df099bf384f3764c3da189635108bb287c3730b"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-39","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":39,"english":"The blameless daughter of Mithilă protected by the Rākşasis, saw the infuriated Rāvana bearing that best of blades in his hand. Seeing (Ravana) approach, sword in hand, despite the earnest entreaties of his friends, Janaka's daughter, SIta, pained and overcome by grief, said, weeping-From the way in which, wrought up with wrath, this wicked-minded one is rushing on at me personally it appears that he will slay me, who have a lord, as one who has not.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-40"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080039","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"fea6053f695db924a22a62d04ac701bd3b6a2a2c23230437e7e8ea38ed4e0916","compact":"0d0913368c5feefbd2bf0a9fc11d5c761c1deb9477af42366cff47194498901e","nasal":"ea81426494f398cd1fe75c8446162d9c9cffa51de6a3849d3340a2b946738f00"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-40","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":40,"english":"The blameless daughter of Mithilă protected by the Rākşasis, saw the infuriated Rāvana bearing that best of blades in his hand. Seeing (Ravana) approach, sword in hand, despite the earnest entreaties of his friends, Janaka's daughter, SIta, pained and overcome by grief, said, weeping-From the way in which, wrought up with wrath, this wicked-minded one is rushing on at me personally it appears that he will slay me, who have a lord, as one who has not.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.831,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-40"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080040","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9277bb86f7e4be6029bc9c4e010d7c8ca003847479affe6fecc3e98ffd76155e","compact":"43e0b591a8656b3cbba61e4dc031a1f7a18769358f8912e8f315233460f0bafa","nasal":"43e0b591a8656b3cbba61e4dc031a1f7a18769358f8912e8f315233460f0bafa"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-41","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":41,"english":"The blameless daughter of Mithilă protected by the Rākşasis, saw the infuriated Rāvana bearing that best of blades in his hand. Seeing (Ravana) approach, sword in hand, despite the earnest entreaties of his friends, Janaka's daughter, SIta, pained and overcome by grief, said, weeping-From the way in which, wrought up with wrath, this wicked-minded one is rushing on at me personally it appears that he will slay me, who have a lord, as one who has not.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-40"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080041","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"05c90ec47758cf262e4c0275a23088118071a79e848007a511b501d1fa3338f7","compact":"51d61ebdce1ea8617b69223dabb53d5f24e300d2ff00c39e10b5e77a2292aa73","nasal":"51d61ebdce1ea8617b69223dabb53d5f24e300d2ff00c39e10b5e77a2292aa73"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-42","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":42,"english":"He did urge me much ever devoted to my lord saying Be you my wife; but forsooth I disregarded him.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.661,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-41"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080042","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b34ba916e5f42441945ce75e044de1636f06950232b25ac74cf965a7ad99eac3","compact":"a5b9416c6667da5b2dc365f022ed3219705143348ea171ebc87a42218726d1cd","nasal":"f622e5d8172e2086dacac0598c2e5c790aebc1156f9bc7351a1a0f9e910303e2"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-43","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":43,"english":"And plainly in consequence of my refusal, he has given way to despair. And overcome by anger and passion, he is ready to slay me.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.561,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-42"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080043","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5cf7105b32603cd4782b9aa9c7ea4281bb5d3b6923f298a8260250a697b69486","compact":"12020410571d8ca40fd373c86075f01184aca4a54de33763ddc10920a0cccfcb","nasal":"0c2fb9f2e687ae4a26172456a6fb521218b18f76fd63b152aa4da51b8b7fb60e"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-44","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":44,"english":"Or it may be that today for me those foremost of men, the brothers-Rama and Laksmana-have been slain in encounter by this inglorious one. Alas! O fie! For me those princes have met with destruction. Or it may be that not without slaying Rāma and Laksmana, this terrific Rākşasa having his heart set on sin, from grief for his son will slay me.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.619,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-43","DUTT-6-92-45"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080044","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"03df7cddd796c99a50560a33126f3a498ffcbad271d5b212fc2cb261782d97f7","compact":"1e04962558f32e2daa9e7aa8cfee7bc149047d81be503a64703a09849921687f","nasal":"ce40ccaf553c30fcb301a9befa931ee9da68aa053dd667362378b04ddf66ff28"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-45","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":45,"english":"Fool that I am I did not then act agreeably to Hanumān's words. If I had gone away seated on his back, without waiting for the victory (of my lord). I devoted to him placed on his lap, should not have come by this repentance.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.711,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-46"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080045","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b139d778c78c21488ec5dc96f1d0f7ee114f48b54f4f8eadae64ab61e16cb49a","compact":"81d8ac12d0a7b95b3b4c4e61f0b60e964620382dd4447ddd48dcf8c91deafebd","nasal":"fe39712f8533bd3e1d5ef5978713eda174df358b7c82d7dd3097a58f27352875"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-46","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":46,"english":"When Kausalya, having an only son, will hear of her son slain in battle, I fancy, her heart shall burst.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-47"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080046","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6e9afc6f38e696de6ba023fa1ed12a102a9bfdccaf2132b39d52b288c0d87559","compact":"c9c621f43e4bd6a9f49ae2ac17ffb629721ce75f7c23195fe23e8dba32fbb5e1","nasal":"c9c621f43e4bd6a9f49ae2ac17ffb629721ce75f7c23195fe23e8dba32fbb5e1"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-47","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":47,"english":"And weeping she will remember that highsouled one's birth and weeping, she will remember that high-souled one's birth and boyhood, and youth and pious acts and loveliness.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.86,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-48"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080047","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"66671221aaaa9a0b5dd250529ee801a69b9a73859c7ea1a6450b3d0f425b9de0","compact":"274bc2091a324f2b14e1785cf5b853fa551a2af13ade04cfa2a3189c11bdbf76","nasal":"274bc2091a324f2b14e1785cf5b853fa551a2af13ade04cfa2a3189c11bdbf76"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-48","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":48,"english":"That one bereft of her consciousness, filled with despair, after having performed his srāddha,* will doubtless enter into fire or water. *Funereal rites performed in accordance with the injunctions of the Hindu Scriptures.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.882,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-49"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080048","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"301dd1033534dad9694efb1ea89b895d94626253d7d332eeb82638bd9a4c9c40","compact":"0a043c0171c8e075b71699167bd6b6fdc4ef1dc63fd75050c19f479ded2a4c13","nasal":"0a043c0171c8e075b71699167bd6b6fdc4ef1dc63fd75050c19f479ded2a4c13"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-49","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":49,"english":"Shame on the unchaste and hump-backed Manthara, having her heart fixed in sin, for whose sake this grief shat Rāvana's exercise Kausalya!","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.58,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-50"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080049","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3daaf35f1363976796637118ffd38798378f4f7982543f57621bdbbc85822883","compact":"8798f5c5148829bd9dbd26f801ded30f9e46eb5e7259011bcdfd75818e2704f6","nasal":"c4c58383d3240d3ce9f36317cbfeb9989d473e13b6b8f68008406e83363bf0c5"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-50","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":50,"english":"Seeing the distressed daughter of Mithila lament thus, like to Rohiņi come under the sway of a planet in the absence of the Moon, in the meanwhile his (Rāvana's) pure and good courtier, named Suparsva, endowed with intelligence, albeit prevented by other courtiers, spoke to Rāvana, foremost of Räkșasa.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.881,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-51"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080050","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"51e03c7ece1c09972e1cc5d5f0444bab120d88a641b87b6b42986c07f56350f4","compact":"76b82c51ad22aaacf824a1a8c8e2dca4fee925f8e8697bb568ab5491dc1eb7e9","nasal":"10fd045987b566ac552c297982c2d020803cbc58d03108beaafb2a6813c05a57"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-51","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":51,"english":"Seeing the distressed daughter of Mithila lament thus, like to Rohiņi come under the sway of a planet in the absence of the Moon, in the meanwhile his (Rāvana's) pure and good courtier, named Suparsva, endowed with intelligence, albeit prevented by other courtiers, spoke to Rāvana, foremost of Räkșasa.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.475,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-51"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080051","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9514e91c19295ff3d844574d4dc9934076470cc68d3a5e6e99b3f810bc8d7a16","compact":"adcf09bdcfbdfa2664f580cc75250e1e1cc6111448fd8e335d8d9ad5da6ba6d9","nasal":"adcf09bdcfbdfa2664f580cc75250e1e1cc6111448fd8e335d8d9ad5da6ba6d9"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-52","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":52,"english":"Wherefore, O Ten-necked one, O you who are the very younger brother of Vaiśravaņa himself, Did you desire to slay Vaidehī, forsaking righteousness?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.844,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-52"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080052","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c94f2dd26669147256165a066c6693ea1e820d72eb0333913686bee89f0be6a4","compact":"6dafc281780440edd47edb6c2b9b77e6f1ed83d2e06bf2f42cc7276ff1914046","nasal":"a58e7ee8eab733a0a3f6c989c805467a459463de66f673be6e029388dbede58e"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-53","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":53,"english":"Why O hero! O lord of Rākşasas! having led the Brahmacarya mode of life, studied the Vedas, and returned from the house of your spiritual guide, and ever engaged in religious acts, today did you intend to slay a woman?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.606,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-53"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080053","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c6ee43d905de708def2eb63b573fefcad1e5d8b02fde107500b4824438de19f1","compact":"90a20f1465e600e71548bd176fbf153626c1b20604f8a0646d98c252d0b3c9bb","nasal":"90a20f1465e600e71548bd176fbf153626c1b20604f8a0646d98c252d0b3c9bb"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-54","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":54,"english":"Do you, O king, now stay expecting Mithila's daughter' endowed with loveliness; and do you along with us vent your wrath on him.\" 1. Explains the commentator: till you slay Rāma in battle. 2. Rama.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.515,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-54"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080054","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"da11768ab4e9e60f0795c1975fe927177a9cb1ad1da127c32e18300cd5eae81e","compact":"b4b542472de5a504a2ea02f8ef57d6b336947b1be5330e9f2875f758afc4c7bf","nasal":"e2cbd23b90e4945f64584cd1ff7d3079bb2c055b033f8735dd12eb006642e8af"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-55","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":55,"english":"To-day being the fourteenth day of the dark fortnight, do you this very day prepare for conflict; at amăvāsyā,* environed with your forces do you march for victory. *i.e. tomorrow.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.873,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-55"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080055","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e7f968653e5fe377d9cbbd222f0990995fab2c5637998716495c5af6573d96c9","compact":"83d0fc207b5098c0eeca8e6ab63569dcf75a3d9ea4bc38964cbad208d2a4ecc1","nasal":"83d0fc207b5098c0eeca8e6ab63569dcf75a3d9ea4bc38964cbad208d2a4ecc1"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-56","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":56,"english":"Heroic, and intelligent, a mighty car-warrior and swordsman, you, mounted on a car, slay the redoubtable son of Dasaratha, will obtain Mithilā's daughter.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.652,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-56"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080056","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2a759d4f1af35a5bc16f7016cb0b2c54b07e873f887ccc9e75f8d842eaddddf9","compact":"16fb16b32e5a3ea92c9340dae32815da986e113f21fabe87681a801c9722998d","nasal":"16fb16b32e5a3ea92c9340dae32815da986e113f21fabe87681a801c9722998d"}},{"id":"VRCE-6-80-57","kanda":6,"kandaId":"yuddha","sarga":80,"verse":57,"english":"Thus exhorted by his friend, the untighteous Rāvana accepted his virtuous speech; and then that powerful one went (back) to his house, and, surrounded by his friends, entered the court.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.842,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-6-92-57"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"6080057","sanskritFile":"Ram06.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d9adceaa8e6c292af475ea9dea1a0a5c3561e8a1e34b3e71943423530c934ab4","compact":"278c492438cbe342dfae92981e703a1c8c8bd546370bb57457a3d13dca4c32b0","nasal":"278c492438cbe342dfae92981e703a1c8c8bd546370bb57457a3d13dca4c32b0"}}]
